arXiv:2603.29761v1 [cs.AI] 31 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneck
<br class="ltx_break">in Searchless Chess Transformers</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Quanhao Li
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Abbey Park High School
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">heinzliyuanhao@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wei Jiang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Shanghai Soong Ching Ling School
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">chesssolveasy@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">March 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A human-like chess engine should mimic the style, errors, and consistency of a strong human player rather than maximize playing strength.
We show that training such an engine from move sequences alone forces the model to learn two capabilities simultaneously—<em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">state tracking</em> (reconstructing the board from the move history) and <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decision quality</em> (choosing good moves given that knowledge)—and that these capabilities impose contradictory demands on training data.
Low-rated games supply the positional diversity essential for tracking; high-rated games supply the quality signal for decision learning.
Filtering out low-rated data, the intuitive route to better moves, consistently degrades performance: illegal-move rates double and middlegame accuracy collapses.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We formalize this tension as a <em id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual-capability bottleneck</em>, <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, where performance is limited by whichever capability is weaker.
Guided by this framework, we (i) scale the model from 28 M to 120 M parameters to repair tracking, then (ii) introduce <em id="abstract1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Elo-weighted training</em>—reducing the gradient contribution of low-rated games without removing them—to boost decision quality while preserving diversity.
A complete <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> factorial ablation supports the view that scaling primarily fixes tracking while weighting primarily improves decisions, and their combination produces the strongest practical-play gains.
A weighting sweet spot exists: linear weighting (<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>) achieves the optimal balance, while exponential weighting (<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>) destroys tracking despite achieving lower validation loss.
We further introduce a <em id="abstract1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coverage-decay formula</em>, <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{*}=\log(N/k_{\mathrm{crit}})/\log b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>crit</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{*}=\log(N/k_{\mathrm{crit}})/\log b</annotation></semantics></math>, as a characteristic reliability horizon for intra-game degeneration risk.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">Our final model (120 M parameters, no search) reaches Lichess bullet <span id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2570</span> over 253 rated games—to our knowledge, the strongest reported pure move-sequence chess model (top 1% of Lichess bullet players).
Under native-input evaluation, it achieves 55.2% Top-1 accuracy on human move prediction, compared to 50.0% (Maia-2 rapid) and 50.4% (Maia-2 blitz); part of this advantage reflects the richer sequence input rather than model quality alone.
The model plays on Lichess without triggering the platform’s anti-cheating system, consistent with human-like move and timing patterns.
Unlike position-based methods, the sequence input naturally encodes full game history, enabling history-dependent decision-making that single-position models structurally cannot exhibit.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>The goal</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Superhuman chess engines have existed for over two decades, yet for most players they are practically useless as training partners.
When a 1500-rated player asks Stockfish why their move was wrong, the engine suggests a line involving a quiet rook maneuver whose purpose only becomes clear twelve moves later—a sequence no human below master level could conceive, let alone execute.
The gap between superhuman play and human understanding is not a feature; it is a barrier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">What players need is a <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relatable</em> opponent: an engine that plays like a human slightly above their level—stylistically coherent across an entire game, capable of occasional inaccuracies that mirror real human tendencies, yet never blundering material through sheer positional confusion.
The goal is not to maximize playing strength but to maximize <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">human-likeness</em> at a target skill level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work has made significant progress.
Maia and Maia-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">McIlroy-Young et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> predict human moves with impressive accuracy by training on board positions at various skill levels.
However, these models operate on single board states (FEN) and have no access to game history—they cannot distinguish the same position reached via an aggressive gambit from one reached via quiet maneuvering, and therefore cannot maintain stylistic coherence across a game.
At the other end, attempts to repurpose general-purpose LLMs for chess <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> treat move sequences as text but achieve only modest playing strength (<math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1788" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1788</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1788</annotation></semantics></math> Elo), far below strong amateur level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we build a searchless autoregressive Transformer that learns exclusively from move sequences—with no board representation, no search, and no hand-crafted chess knowledge—and plays bullet chess at the level of a 2500+ rated human.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Why this is hard: the tracking–decision contradiction</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model is a standard decoder-only Transformer whose input is a sequence of moves in UCI notation; it predicts the next move via next-token prediction.
Crucially, it never sees the board.
Every piece’s location must be implicitly reconstructed from the move history—a 64-square board state inferred from a growing sequence of algebraic tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This places two simultaneous demands on the model:</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">State tracking (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The model must maintain an accurate internal representation of the current board position—which pieces occupy which squares, what has been captured, whether castling rights remain.
Tracking failure manifests as illegal moves: attempts to move pieces that are not where the model believes them to be.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decision quality (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an accurate board representation, the model must select moves consistent with human-level positional and tactical understanding.
We measure decision quality through centipawn loss (CPL) and move-prediction accuracy on high-rated subsets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The central difficulty is that these two capabilities require <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contradictory</em> training data:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tracking</span> is trained by <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">diversity</em> of positions. Low-rated players make unconventional moves that produce unusual piece configurations rarely seen in high-level play. These “messy” positions are the training signal the model needs for robust board reconstruction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decision quality</span> is trained by <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quality</em> of moves. High-rated players choose principled moves that embody sound positional understanding.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The naive approach—filtering out low-rated games—consistently fails: illegal-move rates double, middlegame and endgame accuracy collapse, and the filtered model loses decisively to the unfiltered baseline in head-to-head play (<a href="#S4" title="4 The Diversity–Quality Paradox ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
A single tracking failure (blundering a piece) is catastrophically more costly than any number of slightly suboptimal but legal moves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize this as a <em id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bottleneck</em>:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\;\leq\;\min(T,\,Q)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\;\leq\;\min(T,\,Q),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is effective playing strength.
Performance is limited by whichever capability is weaker.
<a href="#S1.F1" title="In Decision quality (𝑄). ‣ 1.2 Why this is hard: the tracking–decision contradiction ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the complete variable structure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29761v1/fig1_variable_structure.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/329;" width="476" height="329" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Core variable relationships. Two controllable levers (model capacity and data weighting) feed two capabilities (tracking <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and decision quality <math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>), governed by the bottleneck <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>A chain of failures leading to a solution</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experimental path was driven by successive failure diagnoses (<a href="#S1.F2" title="In 1.3 A chain of failures leading to a solution ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29761v1/fig2_experimental_chain.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/566;" width="476" height="566" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Experimental causal chain. Each step’s failure diagnosis motivates the next intervention, from baseline through filtering failure, model scaling, Elo weighting, and the discovery of the weighting sweet spot.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline</span> (28 M, all Elo): <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}2000</annotation></semantics></math> Lichess bullet, but quality degrades as games progress. Illegal rate: 1.06%.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Filtering</span> (28 M, high Elo only): illegal rate nearly doubles to 1.82%; middlegame <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-3.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-3.6</annotation></semantics></math> pp, endgame <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-6.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-6.4</annotation></semantics></math> pp. Loses to baseline. <em id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Diagnosis: <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> crashed, became new bottleneck.</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scaling</span> (28 M <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 120 M, uniform): illegal rate drops to 0.22%, Top-1 rises to 50.85%, but only modest head-to-head gains (80–54 over 200 games, <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}0.03</annotation></semantics></math>). <em id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Diagnosis: <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> still bottlenecked.</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Elo-weighted training</span> (120 M, linear <math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>): slightly higher Top-1 (51.2% vs. 50.85%) at a small tracking cost (0.26% vs. 0.22% illegal), but dominates V2.0 41–24 and baseline 66–8–26. Lichess bullet <span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2570</span>. <em id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sweet spot reached.</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Over-weighting</span> (120 M, exponential <math id="S1.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>): lower validation loss but weaker play; endgame collapses, Elo conditioning breaks. <em id="S1.I2.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Diagnosis: <math id="S1.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> re-emerges as bottleneck.</em></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The dual-capability bottleneck.</span> We identify and formalize the tracking–decision tradeoff: <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, explaining why filtering low-quality data consistently degrades sequence-based chess models (<a href="#S4" title="4 The Diversity–Quality Paradox ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Elo-weighted training and full factorial ablation.</span> We propose Elo-weighted loss as a principled resolution: it boosts <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving the diversity that <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> requires. A complete <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> factorial (two scales <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> two weighting regimes) supports the view that scaling primarily fixes tracking while weighting primarily improves decisions, and their combination produces the strongest practical-play gains (<a href="#S5" title="5 The Weighting Sweet Spot ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intra-game degeneration analysis.</span> We introduce per-game degeneration points and the coverage-decay horizon <math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{*}=\log(N/k_{\mathrm{crit}})/\log b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>crit</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{*}=\log(N/k_{\mathrm{crit}})/\log b</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that degeneration is localized within games and delayed by stronger models (<a href="#S6" title="6 Intra-Game Degeneration and the Aligned Cliff ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A competitive human-like engine from pure move sequences.</span> Our final model (120 M, no search) reaches Lichess bullet 2570 (top 1%), outperforms Maia-2 under native-input evaluation by 5 pp on human move prediction, shows stronger alignment with human error patterns, and exhibits history-dependent decision-making that FEN-only models structurally cannot exhibit (<a href="#S7" title="7 Evaluation of Human-Likeness ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Superhuman chess engines</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dominant paradigm combines neural evaluation with explicit tree search.
AlphaZero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silver et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and Leela Chess Zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leela Chess Zero Contributors, 2026</a>)</cite> use convolutional networks with MCTS, trained via self-play reinforcement learning.
Stockfish <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stockfish Developers, 2023</a>)</cite> pairs NNUE evaluation with alpha-beta search.
These systems achieve superhuman strength but are fundamentally unsuitable for human-like behavior: they model how to win, not how humans think.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A notable departure is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruoss et al. (2024)</a></cite>, who train a 270 M Transformer to predict Stockfish action-values from FEN, achieving Lichess blitz 2895 without search.
However, this is a distillation of Stockfish—its errors bear no resemblance to human errors, and its FEN-only input encodes no game history.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Human-like chess engines</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Maia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">McIlroy-Young et al., 2020</a>)</cite> pioneered human-move prediction by training separate Leela-architecture models for each Elo bracket.
Maia-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduced a skill-aware attention mechanism on a ResNet backbone, producing a single unified model conditioned on player Elo.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two limitations of the Maia line are relevant here.
First, both operate on single board positions (FEN): two games reaching the same position via different paths produce identical predictions, precluding game-level style coherence.
Second, neither investigates <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data composition</em>—whether to include or exclude low-rated games, or how the quality–diversity balance affects model capabilities.
As we show, these questions are critical.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Sequence models and implicit state tracking</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Othello-GPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> demonstrated that a GPT trained on move sequences develops an internal world model, confirmed by linear probes on hidden states.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toshniwal et al. (2022)</a></cite> systematically studied the conditions under which Transformers learn state tracking from chess move sequences, finding that full attention and sufficient data volume are necessary.
However, they did not investigate how <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data composition</em>—the balance between low-rated and high-rated games—affects tracking quality, nor did they identify any tension between tracking and decision capabilities.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen (2024)</a></cite> extended the emergent world-model finding to chess, showing that a 50 M GPT trained on PGN sequences learns 64-square board representations and implicit Elo estimation, achieving <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1500</annotation></semantics></math> Elo.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">ChessGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and ChessLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> fine-tune general-purpose LLMs on chess data, with ChessLLM reaching <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1788" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1788</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1788</annotation></semantics></math> Elo.
General-purpose LLMs dedicate most parameters to linguistic capabilities, leaving limited effective capacity for board tracking.
Neither paper analyzes why performance plateaus or investigates training data composition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, our work is the first to (i) demonstrate that a dedicated sequence model can reach strong human-level play (2500+), and (ii) systematically study how data composition affects tracking and decision quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Architecture and input representation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use a standard decoder-only Transformer with Pre-RMSNorm, rotary position embeddings (RoPE), and SwiGLU activations at two scales (<a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.1 Architecture and input representation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Model configurations.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Small</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Large</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Parameters</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">28 M</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">120 M</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Layers</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">12</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">16</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Hidden dim</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">384</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">768</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Attention heads</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">12</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">FFN dim (SwiGLU)</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1536</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3072</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The input is a token sequence:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\texttt{[BOS]}\;\texttt{[time\_control]}\;\texttt{[elo]}\;\texttt{[color]}\;\text{move}_{1}\;\text{move}_{2}\;\cdots\;\text{move}_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[BOS]</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[time_control]</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[elo]</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[color]</mtext><mtext>move</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext>move</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo><mtext>move</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{[BOS]}\;\texttt{[time\_control]}\;\texttt{[elo]}\;\texttt{[color]}\;\text{move}_{1}\;\text{move}_{2}\;\cdots\;\text{move}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where each move is in UCI notation (<span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">e2e4</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">g1f3</span>).
The vocabulary contains <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1{,}900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1,900</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1{,}900</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.
The model has no access to any board representation; piece positions must be inferred entirely from the move history.
At inference, the model generates moves via temperature-controlled sampling with no search.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Training details</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models are trained for 2 epochs with AdamW (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}{=}0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}{=}0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}{=}0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}{=}0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>), learning rate <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> with cosine decay to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size 2048, warmup of 4000 steps, gradient clipping at 1.0, dropout <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and bf16 mixed precision.
Maximum sequence length is 170 tokens (P99.5 of the data distribution).
The 28 M models train in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}2.5</annotation></semantics></math> hours on a single NVIDIA H100; the 120 M models train in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}70</annotation></semantics></math> hours.
All models use Flash Attention 2 and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.compile</span> for throughput optimization (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}134{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>134,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}134{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> tokens/s on H100).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Training data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All data is sourced from the Lichess open database, spanning the full rating range (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}600</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2800</annotation></semantics></math> Elo) in Bullet and Blitz time controls.
Each game produces two training sequences (one per color).
We additionally include Lichess puzzle data (1–5.8 M puzzles) as supplementary sequences.
No Elo-based filtering is applied by default.
<a href="#S3.T2" title="In 3.3 Training data ‣ 3 Method ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a complete overview of all model variants discussed in this paper.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Overview of all model variants. Entries marked with <math id="S3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math> use Elo-filtered data rather than all-Elo data.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Params</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weighting</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Illegal</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role in paper</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">V1.0</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">28 M</td>
