</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Elo-weighted training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than selecting which games to include, we modulate gradient contributions via sample-level loss weighting:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}w(e_{i})\,\ell(x_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}w(e_{i})\,\ell(x_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross-entropy loss for sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the average Elo of the two players.
We define the <em id="S3.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weighting intensity</em> <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=w(e_{\max})/w(e_{\min})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=w(e_{\max})/w(e_{\min})</annotation></semantics></math> and investigate three schemes (<a href="#S3.T4" title="In 3.4 Elo-weighted training ‣ 3 Method ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Weighting schemes.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scheme</span></th>
<th id="S3.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function</span></th>
<th id="S3.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S3.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-Elo gradient</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Uniform</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(e)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(e)=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{:}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{:}1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}5\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Linear</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(e)=\mathrm{clip}(\alpha(e-e_{\min}),\,w_{\min},\,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(e)=\mathrm{clip}(\alpha(e-e_{\min}),\,w_{\min},\,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}20{:}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}20{:}1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}45\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Exponential</th>
<td id="S3.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(e)=\exp(\beta(e-e_{\mathrm{ref}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(e)=\exp(\beta(e-e_{\mathrm{ref}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}200{:}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>200</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}200{:}1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}85\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Analysis framework: tracking and decision quality</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A sequence model predicting chess moves must implicitly solve two subproblems:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">State tracking (<math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reconstructing the board from the move sequence.
We measure <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> primarily via the <em id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">illegal-move rate</em>: each illegal move is a direct, unambiguous signal of tracking failure.
We additionally use linear probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen, 2024</a>)</cite> on hidden states to measure internal board-representation accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decision quality (<math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Selecting human-like moves given an (approximately) accurate board representation.
We measure <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> through CPL, move-prediction accuracy on high-rated subsets, and Stockfish agreement rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These jointly determine playing strength.
A model with perfect <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> but poor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> plays legal but weak moves.
A model with strong <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> but degraded <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> periodically blunders material—and a single piece blunder typically decides the game.
We formalize this as the bottleneck in <a href="#S1.E1" title="In Decision quality (𝑄). ‣ 1.2 Why this is hard: the tracking–decision contradiction ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The Diversity–Quality Paradox</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The natural approach to improving playing strength is to filter out low-rated games and train only on high-quality data.
We show that this intuition is not merely wrong but <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">predictably</em> wrong: filtering consistently degrades performance, and the mechanism reveals the dual-capability structure.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Why tracking and decision quality require opposing data</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective diversity</em> of dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> under weighting <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of distinct board states receiving at least <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> units of weighted exposure:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Div}(D)=\bigl|\{s:s\text{ appears in }D\text{ at least }\theta\text{ times}\}\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> appears in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> at least </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> times</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Div}(D)=\bigl|\{s:s\text{ appears in }D\text{ at least }\theta\text{ times}\}\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective quality</em> is the weighted average move quality:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Qual}(D,w)=\frac{\sum_{e}w(e)\,n_{e}\,q(e)}{\sum_{e}w(e)\,n_{e}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Qual</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Qual}(D,w)=\frac{\sum_{e}w(e)\,n_{e}\,q(e)}{\sum_{e}w(e)\,n_{e}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(e)</annotation></semantics></math> is the average move quality (inverse CPL) at rating <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Low-rated data contributes high diversity (unusual positions from unconventional play) but low quality.
High-rated data contributes high quality but narrow diversity (standard openings, typical structures).
Filtering removes the former: <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Qual}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Qual</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Qual}</annotation></semantics></math> rises, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Div}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Div</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Div}</annotation></semantics></math> crashes, and under <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math> the bottleneck switches from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.T5" title="In 4.1 Why tracking and decision quality require opposing data ‣ 4 The Diversity–Quality Paradox ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Predicted effect of filtering under the dual-capability bottleneck.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Div</th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Qual</th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T5.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">All Elo (before)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">High</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Low</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">High</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Low</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Limited by <math id="S4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">High Elo only (after)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow\!\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mpadded><mo>⁣</mo><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow\!\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow\!\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mpadded><mo>⁣</mo><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow\!\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Limited by <math id="S4.T5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.T5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> worse</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experimental validation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare two 28 M models trained on datasets of comparable size:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V1.0</span> (baseline): 9.6 M Lichess games spanning all Elo levels (<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}20</annotation></semantics></math> M sequences, <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1.4</annotation></semantics></math> B tokens, 2 epochs).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V1.1</span> (filtered): retaining only Bullet 2500+ and Blitz 2200+ games, supplemented with tracking patches and puzzles (<math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}25.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>25.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}25.8</annotation></semantics></math> M sequences, 2 epochs).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Filtering experiment: V1.0 (all Elo) vs. V1.1 (filtered).</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V1.0</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V1.1</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretation</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Illegal-move rate</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.06%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.82% (+72%)</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> degraded sharply</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Opening Top-1</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">50.0%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">53.8% (+3.8 pp)</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> improved marginally</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Middlegame Top-1</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">43.5%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">39.9% (<math id="S4.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-3.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-3.6</annotation></semantics></math> pp)</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Middlegame collapsed</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Endgame Top-1</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">46.8%</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">40.4% (<math id="S4.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-6.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-6.4</annotation></semantics></math> pp)</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Endgame collapsed</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Head-to-head (100 games)</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">—</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">V1.0 wins</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> decreased</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results match the theoretical prediction point by point.
The asymmetry of damage is striking: a 3.8 pp improvement in opening accuracy is vastly outweighed by 3.6–6.4 pp degradation in middlegame and endgame—precisely the phases where tracking is most challenged.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-evaluation on V1.0’s validation set confirms the result is not an artifact of distribution shift: validation loss worsens from 1.75 to 2.01, Top-1 accuracy drops from 46.5% to 41.3%, and illegal rate rises from 1.06% to 2.49%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling alone does not rescue filtering.
A 58 M model trained on pure 2000+ data performs worse than V1.0 on both Top-1 accuracy (40.3% vs. 46.5%) and illegal rate (2.68% vs. 1.06%), losing the direct match 0–9–11.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Direct evidence from internal representations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To move beyond behavioral metrics, we train linear board probes on the hidden states of V1.0 and V1.1.
The probe dataset contains 315,606 positions from natural bullet decision points (252,967 train / 31,965 validation / 30,674 test), with 278,760 non-standard and 36,846 standard positions.
For each layer, an independent linear probe predicts one of 13 labels (empty, six white piece types, six black piece types) for each of the 64 squares.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29761v1/fig3_probe_layerwise.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/281;" width="476" height="281" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Layer-wise board-state probe accuracy. The all-Elo base model (V1.0) maintains higher square accuracy throughout the network, with both models peaking in the final layer.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29761v1/fig4_probe_slices.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/187;" width="476" height="187" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Probe accuracy by evaluation slice. The gap between V1.0 and V1.1 is smallest on standard and opening positions, and largest on non-standard, middlegame, and endgame slices—precisely where low-Elo games contribute the most diverse positions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Results (<a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.3 Direct evidence from internal representations ‣ 4 The Diversity–Quality Paradox ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Direct evidence from internal representations ‣ 4 The Diversity–Quality Paradox ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>):</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Best overall square accuracy: 93.40% (V1.0) vs. 92.29% (V1.1).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On <em id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">standard</em> positions: nearly identical (97.57% vs. 97.51%).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On <em id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-standard</em> positions: substantial gap (92.78% vs. 91.45%).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gap widens further in middlegame (92.36% vs. 91.08%) and endgame (92.08% vs. 90.41%).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The probes upgrade the filtering argument from behavioral correlation to <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mechanistic evidence</em>: removing low-Elo data degrades the internal board representation precisely where broad positional coverage matters most.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Summary</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq\min(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq\min(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, any strategy that sacrifices positional diversity to improve move quality will shift the bottleneck from <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Filtering is the most extreme form: it hard-deletes samples, causing irreversible diversity loss.
The problem is not the <em id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">degree</em> of filtering but the <em id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">kind</em> of operation.
This motivates the question: can we reduce the influence of low-quality moves on decision learning without removing the positions they generate from tracking training?</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>The Weighting Sweet Spot</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Weighting versus filtering: a qualitative difference</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Filtering removes low-rated samples entirely: <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{filtered}}=\{(x,e)\in D:e&gt;e_{\mathrm{thresh}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>filtered</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>thresh</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{filtered}}=\{(x,e)\in D:e&gt;e_{\mathrm{thresh}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Every position exclusive to low-rated games contributes exactly zero to tracking training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Weighting retains all samples but modulates gradient contributions:
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}w(e_{i})\,\nabla\ell(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}w(e_{i})\,\nabla\ell(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under linear weighting with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>, low-Elo data contributes <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total gradient, compared to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}70\%</annotation></semantics></math> under uniform weighting and 0% under filtering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The critical insight is that weighting affects <em id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> capabilities simultaneously:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> increases with <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>: the gradient becomes dominated by high-rated samples.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> decreases with <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>: gradient contribution from diverse low-rated positions diminishes.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Weighting is therefore not monotonically beneficial.
There must exist an intermediate <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> where the two capabilities are balanced.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Theoretical formulation of the sweet spot</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> denote the weighting intensity.
In log-space, we approximate each capability’s dependence on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as linear:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T(r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=T_{0}-\alpha\ln r," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=T_{0}-\alpha\ln r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Q_{0}+\beta\ln r," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Q_{0}+\beta\ln r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0},Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0},Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are baselines under uniform weighting (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> capture sensitivity to weighting.
The empirical finding <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}&gt;Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}&gt;Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects that uniform weighting yields serviceable tracking but weak decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\min(T(r),Q(r))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\min(T(r),Q(r))</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimum is at the crossover:</p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}=\exp\!\biggl(\frac{T_{0}-Q_{0}}{\alpha+\beta}\biggr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}=\exp\!\biggl(\frac{T_{0}-Q_{0}}{\alpha+\beta}\biggr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dependence on model capacity.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> depend on model size <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Larger models have higher <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> (better tracking baseline) and lower <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(C)</annotation></semantics></math> (less sensitive to reduced tracking signal), shifting the sweet spot rightward: <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> increases with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, larger models tolerate more aggressive weighting.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Empirical validation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We distinguish three regimes across the available experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unweighted regime (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both V1.0 (28 M) and V2.0 (120 M) use uniform weighting.
V1.0 reaches <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}2000</annotation></semantics></math> Lichess bullet with 1.06% illegal rate; V2.0 improves to 0.22% illegal rate and 50.85% Top-1, but gains only modestly in head-to-head play (80–54 over 200 games vs. V1.0).
The consistent pattern across both scales is that decision quality, rather than tracking, limits practical performance under uniform weighting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">At the sweet spot (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{\approx}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{\approx}20</annotation></semantics></math>, V2.2, 120 M).</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Linear Elo weighting with 20:1 ratio on 50 M all-Elo games (<a href="#S5.T7" title="In At the sweet spot (𝑟≈20, V2.2, 120 M). ‣ 5.3 Empirical validation ‣ 5 The Weighting Sweet Spot ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>V2.2 performance (120 M, linear weighting, <math id="S5.T7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></th>
<th id="S5.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T7.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T7.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T7.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Illegal-move rate</th>
<td id="S5.T7.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.26%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T7.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T7.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Top-1 accuracy</th>
<td id="S5.T7.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">51.2%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T7.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T7.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Lichess bullet Elo</th>
<td id="S5.T7.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2570 (253 games)</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T7.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T7.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">vs. V1.0 (100 games)</th>
<td id="S5.T7.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">66W–8L–26D</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> reach high levels simultaneously.
Diagnosis: <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\approx Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\approx Q</annotation></semantics></math>—we are at or near the sweet spot.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Right of the sweet spot (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{\approx}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{\approx}200</annotation></semantics></math>, V2.3, 120 M).</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exponential weighting with 200:1 ratio on 100 M games (<a href="#S5.T8" title="In Right of the sweet spot (𝑟≈200, V2.3, 120 M). ‣ 5.3 Empirical validation ‣ 5 The Weighting Sweet Spot ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>V2.2 (linear, <math id="S5.T8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{\approx}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{\approx}20</annotation></semantics></math>) vs. V2.3 (exponential, <math id="S5.T8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{\approx}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{\approx}200</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T8.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T8.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T8.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></th>
<th id="S5.T8.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T8.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V2.2</span></th>
<th id="S5.T8.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T8.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V2.3</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T8.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T8.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Validation loss</th>
<td id="S5.T8.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.48</td>
<td id="S5.T8.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1.40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1.40</annotation></semantics></math> (lower)</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T8.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T8.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Practical play</th>
<td id="S5.T8.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Lichess 2570</td>
<td id="S5.T8.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Weaker</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T8.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T8.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Elo conditioning (2600 vs. 1200)</th>
<td id="S5.T8.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Works (27:13)</td>
<td id="S5.T8.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Broken (17:16 <math id="S5.T8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> random)</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T8.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T8.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Endgame quality</th>
<td id="S5.T8.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Normal</td>
<td id="S5.T8.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Collapsed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite achieving lower validation loss, V2.3 plays worse.
The exponential weighting reduced low-Elo gradients to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>—functionally zero—recreating the filtering problem.
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> re-emerged as the bottleneck.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Full factorial ablation: isolating weighting from scaling</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To disentangle the contributions of scaling and weighting, we complete the full <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> factorial design by training V2.0 (120 M, uniform weighting) and V1.0w (28 M, linear weighting), in addition to V1.0 (28 M, uniform) and V2.2 (120 M, linear).
<a href="#S5.T9" title="In 5.4 Full factorial ablation: isolating weighting from scaling ‣ 5 The Weighting Sweet Spot ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> summarizes the offline metrics; <a href="#S5.T10" title="In 5.4 Full factorial ablation: isolating weighting from scaling ‣ 5 The Weighting Sweet Spot ‣ Tracking vs. Deciding: The Dual-Capability Bottleneckin Searchless Chess Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> reports all pairwise head-to-head results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Full <math id="S5.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> factorial: offline metrics. Weighting slightly increases illegal rate (degraded tracking) while improving Top-1 accuracy (better decisions) at both scales.</figcaption>
